<BaseLayout title="PC6 (Neiguan) — Inner Pass Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat PC6 (Neiguan) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "PC6 (Neiguan)", href: "/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2025-02-15" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">PC6 (Neiguan) — Inner Pass</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">PC6, known as Neiguan or Inner Pass, is the premier acupressure point for nausea and anxiety. Located on the inner forearm, it is widely recommended for motion sickness, morning sickness, and chemotherapy-related nausea — and it is the best-researched point on this site.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Pericardium (PC)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Inner forearm, about three of your own finger-widths below the wrist crease, between the two tendons</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nausea and vomiting, motion sickness, morning sickness, anxiety, chest tightness</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy — make a loose fist to see the tendons, then press between them</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds; 2–3 minutes sustained for nausea</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">As needed, or 1–3 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36 (Zusanli)</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a>, <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">HT7 (Shenmen)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">An open wound on the wrist or forearm, or a recent wrist injury or surgery. Safe for pregnancy morning sickness, but check with your provider first.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why PC6 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">No acupressure point on the body has more accumulated research than PC6 has for nausea. It is the point most consistently studied for postoperative, chemotherapy-related, and motion-sickness nausea, and it is widely regarded as the single most useful point to know for queasiness of any cause. In traditional Chinese medicine, PC6 opens the chest and regulates the stomach, which matches its modern use for both nausea and the tight, constricted sensation that often accompanies anxious feelings.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">PC6 sits on the Pericardium meridian, which in the traditional model protects the Heart and influences the upper digestive tract. On a practical level, its position on the inner forearm makes it easy to find, easy to press yourself, and easy to maintain with a wristband that delivers steady pressure — which is why it shows up in travel, hospital, and home routines alike.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/pc6-location.webp" alt="PC6 acupressure point location on the inner forearm" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="600" height="352" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">PC6 is on the inner forearm, approximately 2 cun (about three finger-widths) below the wrist crease, between the two prominent tendons (palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis). It lies on the Pericardium meridian. When you make a loose fist, the two tendons become more visible, which makes the point easier to locate.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it sits between the two visible tendons, not on them; (2) it is about three of your own finger-widths from the wrist crease; (3) pressing it produces a deep, dull ache — if you feel an electric jolt or tingling into the palm, you have drifted off the point.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use PC6 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You feel nauseous, queasy, or carsick</li>
          <li>• You are about to travel and want to prevent motion sickness (press 20–30 minutes before)</li>
          <li>• You are pregnant and dealing with morning sickness (with your provider's OK)</li>
          <li>• You feel anxious, with tightness or pressure in the chest</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• There is a cut, wound, rash, or recent surgery on the wrist or forearm</li>
          <li>• You have severe chest pain, pressure, or breathlessness — this needs emergency care, not acupressure</li>
          <li>• Pressure causes tingling or numbness into the hand</li>
          <li>• You are pregnant: PC6 is safe for morning sickness, but check with your midwife or doctor first</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: PC6 is superb for nausea, but sudden chest pain or pressure is never something to self-treat.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Nausea and vomiting:</strong> The best-researched point on this site — used for motion sickness, morning sickness, and chemotherapy-related nausea.</li>
      <li><strong>Anxiety and stress:</strong> PC6 calms the mind and may help reduce symptoms of panic and general anxiety.</li>
      <li><strong>Post-surgery recovery:</strong> It is commonly used in hospitals to reduce post-operative nausea.</li>
      <li><strong>Chest tightness:</strong> PC6 relieves chest pressure, palpitations, and breathlessness of an anxious kind.</li>
      <li><strong>Digestive upset:</strong> It helps regulate stomach qi and can ease indigestion and hiccups.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage PC6</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">For nausea, use a firmer, more sustained hold of 2–3 minutes with slow, deep breathing — nausea often requires deeper stimulation than anxiety or sleep work.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Hold your arm with the palm facing up.</li>
      <li>Place your opposite thumb on PC6, between the two tendons.</li>
      <li>Apply firm pressure with your thumb while gently rotating your wrist.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds, breathing slowly and deeply.</li>
      <li>Repeat on the other arm as needed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing on the tendons or digging in so hard that you feel an electric jolt into the hand. The point lies between the two tendons, and nausea wants a firm, sustained hold rather than quick rubbing — a wristband with a firm stud can maintain that pressure for hours.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine PC6 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A nausea or anxiety routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Nausea / motion sickness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">PC6 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a> → <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36</a>, starting 20–30 minutes before travel</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Anxiety / tight chest</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">PC6 → <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">HT7</a> → LI4, holding each 60s with slow breathing</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Morning sickness (pregnancy)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">PC6 only, gently, with your provider's OK — avoid LI4 and SP6 during pregnancy</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Post-meal queasiness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">PC6 → ST36 → <a href="/points/cv12-zhongwan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV12</a>, after waiting 30–60 minutes past the meal</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a complete nausea plan, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-nausea" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for nausea</a> page and the <a href="/body/arm-wrist-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">arm &amp; wrist acupressure points</a> body guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Many people feel relief from nausea within 2–5 minutes of sustained PC6 pressure, which is why it is the go-to point for travel sickness. If the point feels very tender, ease off a notch — tenderness in the inner wrist is common.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–3 short sessions per day, anxiety-related chest tightness and frequent queasiness often ease. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for nausea and anxiety, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For persistent anxiety or nausea, keep PC6 as part of a broader plan that may include medical care. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement — persistent vomiting, chest pain, or anxiety that interferes with daily life should be evaluated by a clinician rather than managed with acupressure alone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">PC6 is the best-researched acupressure point on this site. The nausea evidence is unusually strong for a complementary therapy: PC6 stimulation for nausea and vomiting — postoperative, chemotherapy-related, and motion sickness — has been examined in systematic reviews and Cochrane reviews that synthesize dozens of randomized trials. Results are generally positive, with acupressure shown to work alongside standard anti-emetic treatment rather than replacing it.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers still note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated — though the very consistency of the nausea findings across many trials is what makes PC6 stand out. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of strong encouraging signals for nausea, alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn for other symptoms. For the full nausea routine, see our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-nausea" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for nausea</a> guide.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["cochrane-p6-2009", "chen-2022-anxiety"]} note="PC6 is the single best-documented point on this site. The Cochrane review directly studies stimulation at the P6/PC6 point on the inner wrist, and the meta-analysis above covers acupressure for anxiety, PC6's second main use." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Apply gentle pressure — avoid pressing too hard on the tendons or nerves.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have a wrist injury, carpal tunnel syndrome, or recent surgery in the area.</li>
      <li>Do not press over broken skin, cuts, or rashes on the wrist.</li>
      <li>Pregnant women may use PC6 for morning sickness, but consult your healthcare provider first.</li>
      <li>Stop if you experience numbness, tingling, or sharp pain.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Sudden chest pain, pressure, or severe breathlessness — this needs emergency care, not acupressure</li>
        <li>Persistent vomiting, or the inability to keep down fluids</li>
        <li>Blood in vomit, or nausea with a severe headache</li>
        <li>Nausea or anxiety that gets worse or interferes with daily life</li>
        <li>Symptoms that have not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find PC6 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Make a loose fist with your palm facing up — the two tendons on the inner wrist become visible. PC6 sits about three of your own finger-widths above the wrist crease, between those two tendons. Press for a deep, dull ache; an electric jolt means you are off the point.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press PC6 for nausea?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For nausea, hold a firm, steady pressure for 2–3 minutes per wrist with slow, deep breathing, and repeat as needed. For prevention, stimulate the point 20–30 minutes before a known trigger such as travel or a chemotherapy session. A wristband can maintain the pressure for hours.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Do acupressure wristbands actually work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. Wristbands with a hard plastic stud that presses directly into PC6 are more effective than soft versions and maintain constant pressure for hours. They work best for motion sickness and mild nausea, and they are the easiest option while traveling or at work.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I use PC6 for morning sickness during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">PC6 is traditionally considered safe for pregnancy-related nausea and is one of the few points commonly recommended for morning sickness — but check with your midwife or doctor before using it, and avoid points like LI4 and SP6 during pregnancy. Press gently, in short sessions.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using PC6?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for sudden chest pain or severe breathlessness (emergency), persistent vomiting or inability to keep down fluids, blood in vomit, or nausea with a severe headache. PC6 is a well-supported supportive practice — not a substitute for medical care when these warning signs are present.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

